--TEST--
A method reference written as a whole statement is `E0137` with the call as its fix, and one written where a `string` is expected names the call in its help — `rule:expressions/method-reference`
--FILE--
<?nvs
final class Cache {
    public static function flush(): void {
        echo "flushed\n";
    }

    public function count(): int {
        return 0;
    }
}

// The author meant `Cache::flush()`. Without `()` the statement does nothing.
Cache::flush;
Cache $cache = new Cache();
$cache->count(...);
// A reference is a `callable`, so it does not fit a `string`.
string $label = Cache::flush;
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0137]: this statement is a method reference, and it does nothing
   --> %s:13:1
   |
13 | Cache::flush;
   | ^^^^^^^^^^^^ the reference is created here and never used
   = suggestion: call the method: `Cache::flush()`

error[E0137]: this statement is a method reference, and it does nothing
   --> %s:15:1
   |
15 | $cache->count(...);
   | ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ the reference is created here and never used
   = suggestion: call the method: `$cache->count()`

error[E0401]: expected `string`, found `callable(): void`
   --> %s:17:17
   |
17 | string $label = Cache::flush;
   |                 ^^^^^^^^^^^^ this is `callable(): void`
   = help: `Cache::flush` is a reference to the method. To call it, write `Cache::flush()`

error: aborting due to 3 errors
To read what a code means and how to fix it, run `nvs agent show <code>`.
